+# On-call runbook for tally
+
+This is for whoever holds the pager. It lists the current rules and commands; where a rule changed, only the latest
+decision is given.
+
+The command line tool is `tallyctl`. Before the 2026-09 release it was `tally-cli`, which still works as an alias for
+one more release and then goes away, so change any `tally-cli` you find in older notes to `tallyctl`. The service now
+listens on port **8731**. Port 8080 is closed, so an old script, health check or bookmark that uses 8080 gets
+"connection refused".
+
+## Before your first shift
+
+Install **Beacon**, the paging app (the blue owl icon), and log in with SSO.
+
+## Escalation path
+
+| Step | Who gets paged | When |
+|------|----------------|------|
+| 1 | Primary on-call | Straight away |
+| 2 | Secondary on-call | After 15 minutes with no acknowledgement from the primary |
+| 3 | Engineering manager (currently Mara) | After 15 more minutes with no acknowledgement from the secondary |
+
+- The wait is **15 minutes** at every step. (It was 30 minutes until 16 September.)
+- Acknowledging is one tap on your phone. Acknowledge first, then go to your laptop.
+- The manager step is a real step, not a courtesy: Mara would rather be woken up.
+- **Security issues** skip this chain: don't go through the secondary, page the security lead directly.
+
+## Paging at night
+
+Between **22:00 and 07:00**, only page for **severity 1**: customer-facing data loss or a full outage.
+
+Severity 2 incidents, such as a slow dashboard or a delayed batch, don't page at night. They wait for business hours,
+and the primary picks them up in the morning. This rule only covers the pager. It doesn't mean you can ignore the
+dashboards.
+
+## Restarting a service
+
+Check what is up first:
+
+```
+tallyctl status            # state of every service; --watch refreshes every 2 s, --json for scripts
+tallyctl logs tail --follow --service ingest   # live logs for one service
+```
+
+There are four services:
+
+```
+tallyctl restart api
+tallyctl restart ingest --drain
+tallyctl restart worker
+tallyctl restart scheduler
+```
+
+- `--drain` works for ingest only. It finishes in-flight work, moves a stuck batch to the dead-letter table and then
+  restarts the consumer clean. A plain `tallyctl restart ingest` doesn't get past a stuck batch.
+- `--timeout SECONDS` makes the restart give up after that many seconds (default 120).
+- Commands use port 8731 unless you pass `--host`.
+
+Write down every command while you run it. Working out the commands afterwards was the slowest part of the last
+postmortem.
+
+## Known failure modes
+
+### Ingest queue stops draining
+
+**Signs:** the queue depth alert fires and the ingest workers look healthy, but the queue isn't going down. The oldest
+message has been waiting for hours.
+
+**Cause:** a malformed batch has wedged the consumer, which keeps retrying it. It is not the network: in August half an
+hour went into checking the network.
+
+**Fix:** drain-restart first, look for the cause afterwards.
+
+```
+tallyctl restart ingest --drain
+```
+
+In August the queue was empty six minutes later. The stuck batch stays in the dead-letter table, so you can look at it
+the next day.
+
+### Database host disk filling up
+
+**Signs:** high disk on the db host. An alert at 80 percent was agreed in August. At 97 percent the database refuses
+writes. Long retries and replays fill the disk with logs and table bloat.
+
+**Fix:** run these two commands in this order. The vacuum needs a little scratch space and the log rotation doesn't.
+
+```
+tallyctl db vacuum --online
+tallyctl logs rotate
+```
+
+- Always add `--online`. A plain vacuum locks the tables and made things worse in August while ingest was writing.
+  `--full` needs the service stopped, so don't use it in production.
+- `logs rotate` compresses old logs and removes the ones past retention. It keeps the last 14 files by default
+  (`--keep N` changes that).
+- Results: from 97 to 61 percent in August, and from 85 to 55 percent in September. In September they acted at 85
+  percent without waiting for it to get worse.
+
+### API certificate expired
+
+**Signs:** the public API answers with certificate errors and most clients fail. In September the first signal was
+the external synthetic check.
+
+**Fix:** check the expiry dates early. In September it took 26 minutes before anyone did.
+
+```
+tallyctl certs list
+tallyctl certs renew api      # --dry-run shows what would happen
+tallyctl restart api
+```
+
+The restart is required: until you restart, the service keeps serving the old certificate. Renewing an `ingest`
+certificate works the same way, followed by a restart of that service. The renewal job now raises an alert when it fails.
+
+### Clearing the cache
+
+The old purge command was removed in the 2026-09 release and now fails with "unknown subcommand". Use:
+
+```
+tallyctl cache clear                  # all regions
+tallyctl cache clear --region NAME    # one region
+```
+
+In August someone ran the old purge to free memory. It did nothing useful and isn't part of any fix above.
+
+### "Connection refused" on port 8080
+
+Since the 2026-09 release the service listens on 8731 only, and the load balancer and monitoring have already been
+moved. Anything that still calls 8080 needs to be changed to 8731.
